# {{tenant}} build and promotion

Extracted from HQ `.forgejo/workflows/ci.yml`, `ops/ci/scan-publish.sh` and
`ops/ci/artifact.py` (read-only reference, 2026-10-03). The `docker` runner label
selects HQ's host executor, not a Docker daemon. It needs node/npm, buildctl,
python3, trivy, flock, git and kustomize. BuildKit lives at
`tcp://buildkitd.ci.svc.cluster.local:1234`. No cluster credential belongs in CI.
The workflow uses JSON syntax, a YAML subset, for dependency-free structural
validation with Node. The workflow inherits HQ's checkout@v4/upload-artifact@v3 references; resolve and
review immutable action commits in the authorized network session before activation.

## Complete the build contract

`npm test` is a minimal example gate. Replace it with the tenant's real tests
and required installs (use a committed lockfile and `npm ci` when adding deps).
For static sites, the marked Dockerfile builder body is the tenant-specific
part: run your own build and emit `/build/dist`. Preserve the runner contract.
The included builder only copies index.html; it needs no dependency download.
Node includes a zero-dependency Node 22 HTTP example. Add locked dependencies
and compile steps in its builder as needed; keep runtime artifacts in `/app`.

Both bases use mandatory digest arguments. `ops/ci/base-images.json` starts
with EMPTY strings, so CI refuses to build before reviewed pins are recorded.
No pin in this scaffold is a deployable example digest.

In an explicitly authorized network-enabled session, resolve approved bases:

```sh
docker buildx imagetools inspect node:22-alpine
docker buildx imagetools inspect nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:alpine
```

Record each reported `Digest: sha256:...` verbatim as `node` / `nginx` in
`ops/ci/base-images.json`, plus the source tag, resolution date, platform and
approval in your change review. An OCI index pin is valid if it contains the
approved linux/amd64 image; inspect its child manifest too. Re-inspect by digest,
cache the approved linux/amd64 bases, and verify runtime USER and executable
availability. Never derive a digest from a tag string. Update via reviewed PRs.
Node kind only requires `node`; static requires both. The digest arguments
always accompany fixed repository names in FROM, so tag movement cannot change
bytes. Empty arguments intentionally fail closed. Local example:

```sh
NODE_DIGEST=$(node ops/ci/validate.mjs pin node)
NGINX_DIGEST=$(node ops/ci/validate.mjs pin nginx) # static only
docker build --pull=false --network=none \
  --build-arg NODE_DIGEST="$NODE_DIGEST" \
  --build-arg NGINX_DIGEST="$NGINX_DIGEST" \
  --build-arg SOURCE_URL=https://git.example.com/apps/{{tenant}} \
  --build-arg REVISION="$(git rev-parse HEAD)" \
  --build-arg CREATED="$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" \
  -t {{tenant}}:local .
```

Static: `USER 101:101`, port 8080; mount a size-limited emptyDir at `/tmp`
with pod fsGroup 101. Nginx pid plus client/proxy/fastcgi/uwsgi/scgi temp dirs
are under that mount; logs go to stdout/stderr. Its direct entrypoint avoids
upstream startup scripts rewriting `/etc/nginx` on a read-only root. Node:
`USER 1000:1000`, port 3000, no runtime writes; added scratch needs an emptyDir
at `/tmp` with fsGroup 1000. Both require readOnlyRootFilesystem, drop ALL,
allowPrivilegeEscalation false and RuntimeDefault seccomp in deployment.
Verify `/healthz` under these constraints before activation; a Dockerfile
HEALTHCHECK supplements, and does not replace, Kubernetes probes.

```sh
# Static; for node use uid 1000, port 3000 and /app/server.mjs image defaults.
docker run --rm --read-only --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges \
  --tmpfs /tmp:rw,noexec,nosuid,size=64m,uid=101,gid=101 \
  -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 {{tenant}}:local
# From another terminal: curl --fail http://127.0.0.1:8080/healthz
```

## Scan and exact-byte publication

One BuildKit export produces a single linux/amd64 OCI image. The extracted
verifier checks metadata, manifest, config, compressed layers, diff IDs and
layout identity before and after scanning and before publishing. It rejects
fanout, symlinks, orphan blobs and changed content. The stdlib registry client
uploads these exact bytes, authenticating with `REGISTRY_USER=ci-bot` and
`REGISTRY_TOKEN` (secret by name only). It then GETs the published manifest and
compares both response digest and bytes to the scanned identity. No rebuild.

Trivy fails HIGH and CRITICAL, including unfixed issues. The shared vulnerability
DB is refreshed under a lock, copied read-only per job and required to be at
most 24 hours old (UTC UpdatedAt). `GP_TRIVY_OFFLINE=1` skips refresh and still
requires a provisioned fresh DB; absence/staleness fails CI, and local evidence
must say incomplete. The artifact receipt includes database and allowlist
hashes. An SBOM and scan receipts are uploaded even for PRs that pass scanning.
PRs never receive publication/promotion secrets from this workflow.

`.trivyignore.yaml` deliberately uses JSON syntax (a YAML subset) so the
zero-dependency validator can fail closed. Keep the empty list unless reviewed.
An entry format (replace every descriptive value; do not copy as an exception):

```json
{
  "vulnerabilities": [{
    "id": "CVE-YYYY-NNNN",
    "statement": "Reason this scoped finding is temporarily accepted",
    "owner": "Named accountable owner",
    "review": "URL of approving review",
    "expired_at": "YYYY-MM-DD"
  }]
}
```

Trivy uses `statement` for reason and `expired_at` for expiry. Expiry is midnight
UTC at the start of the date; expired, malformed, duplicate or incomplete
entries fail before scanning. Wildcards, ignore-unfixed and severity downgrades
are not supported. Review should justify image-specific scope and removal.

## Dev and manual prod promotion

Only a main push publishes `sha-<full 40-character source SHA>`. The registry
client refuses a different existing SHA-tag digest. A dedicated gitops-bot
`GITOPS_TOKEN` allows HQ's clone → kustomize edit → bot commit → normal push
mechanism. The only edited file is
`clusters/homelab/tenants/{{tenant}}/overlays/dev/kustomization.yaml`.
It records BOTH `newTag` and `digest`. One rejected push permits one rebase and
retry; another rejection fails. No force push, prod edit or cluster access.
Bot identity/repo membership contains the token; no claim of per-repo PAT scope.
The token is supplied by askpass and never placed in a clone URL or git config.

Prod promotion is a MANUAL PR after dev acceptance, not an app rebuild:

1. In an authorized gitops checkout, branch from current main. Read the exact
   dev image entry and its CI scanned/published digest receipt; verify agreement.
2. Copy that entry's full SHA tag AND digest to prod using the command below,
   substituting the exact accepted dev values. Do not resolve a mutable tag.
3. Verify the rendered prod Pod image is the same `@sha256:` as dev; review the
   diff contains only the intended prod image entry. Open the manual PR and
   attach dev acceptance plus the digest receipt. Gitops's seven checks gate it.
4. Merge/push only with the platform owner's activation approval; main can auto-advance
   deploy. After the gate, a human manually syncs prod and observes imageID.

```sh
cd clusters/homelab/tenants/{{tenant}}/overlays/prod
# TAG and DIGEST are copied from the accepted dev entry, never rebuilt.
kustomize edit set image "{{image}}={{image}}:${TAG}@${DIGEST}"
kustomize build .
```

Rollback uses a prior known-good tag+digest through the same reviewed gitops
path. A local commit, successful app CI or uploaded receipt is not deployment
proof. GP scaffolding can consume a verified image pin via `GP_IMAGE_DIGEST`
(or programmatic `planNew({ imageDigest })`); it has no guessed fallback.
